-- title:
--   The cone gap along the axis
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, let $v \in E$ be a unit vector, $\lVert v\rVert = 1$, let $\vartheta \in \mathbb{R}$ and let $t \in \mathbb{R}$. Then
--
--   $$\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,\, t v) \;=\; t \sin\vartheta,$$
--
--   where $\mathrm{gap}(v,\vartheta,p) = \langle v,p\rangle\sin\vartheta - \lVert p - \langle v,p\rangle v\rVert \cos\vartheta$ is the signed distance from $p$ to the boundary of the half-cone with axis $v$ and apex angle $\vartheta$.
--
--   Points on the axis have no transverse component, so the gap reduces to the axial term alone. This is the base case from which the room available inside a cone is computed: a point at axial distance $t$ from the apex has exactly $t\sin\vartheta$ of clearance, and every other estimate in the cone-gap toolkit is a perturbation of this one.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The scalar $t$ is an arbitrary real; for $t < 0$ the point lies on the opposite ray and the gap is correspondingly negative, so the identity records a signed quantity, not a distance. No restriction is placed on $\vartheta$.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/ConeGap.lean#L150-L156

